al basis.)*\n\n## Constructs\n\n*(No constructs accepted yet. Each accepted change appends or rewrites one section here: name,\ngrammar, semantics, one worked example, and a pointer to its `glossary.md` entry and the\nsprint/changelog entry that introduced or last revised it.)*\n\n\nProposed changes available for this translation:\n- [add] TASK: grammar `task_def   ::= "TASK" IDENT "(" [param_list] ")" ":" NEWLINE INDENT step+ DEDENT\nparam_list ::= IDENT ("," IDENT)*\nstep       ::= action | bind | if_stmt | foreach_stmt | task_call | return_stmt\ntask_call  ::= IDENT "(" [arg_list] ")"\narg_list   ::= value ("," value)*\nreturn_stmt::= "RETURN" value\nvalue      ::= STRING | NUMBER | BOOLEAN | IDENT\nIDENT      ::= /[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*/   (reserved words: TASK, IF, ELSE, FOREACH, IN, RETURN, AND, OR, NOT excluded)\nSTRING     ::= "\\"" ... "\\""   NUMBER ::= standard int/float literal   BOOLEAN ::= "true" | "false"\ncomment    ::= "#" up-to-NEWLINE (ignored)` — TASK is the unit of decomposition and reuse: a name, a parameter list, and an ordered, indentation-delimited body of steps, all of which execute unconditionally and strictly top-to-bottom (Python-style flat sequencing — no implicit reordering or parallelism). Calling a TASK (task_call) fully evaluates its argument values first (arguments may only be STRING/NUMBER/BOOLEAN/IDENT — never an inline ACTION or another task_call; that must be BIND\'d first, see BIND), then executes the callee\'s body from its top, then control returns to the call site and resumes the next step. RETURN sets the TASK\'s return value and immediately ends that TASK\'s execution (no steps after RETURN in the same body run); a TASK with no RETURN returns no value. TASK names live in one global namespace, distinct from ACTION verbs and from local variable names (see BIND); a TASK may call itself or other TASKs, including mutual recursion — this is syntactically legal, cycles are not statically rejected, and non-termination is a runtime concern outside the grammar\'s scope. Lexical primitives (identifiers, string/number/boolean literals, `#`-to-end-of-line comments, and the reserved-word set above) are fixed and closed, so parsing never depends on surrounding context.\n- [add] ACTION: grammar `action    ::= VERB "[" [attr_list] "]"\nattr_list ::= attr ("," attr)*\nattr      ::= KEY "=" value\nVERB      ::= IDENT\nKEY       ::= IDENT\nvalue     ::= STRING | NUMBER | BOOLEAN | IDENT` — ACTION is the atomic, effectful primitive: a fixed-form VERB applied to zero or more key=value attributes that carry all situational nuance (recipient, tone, content, etc.), so the core verb vocabulary itself stays small (HTML-style content/attribute separation). Attribute values are restricted to literals or bare identifiers referring to already-BIND\'d variables (see BIND) — an ACTION may never appear inline as another ACTION\'s attribute value; a nested effect must first be captured with BIND and then referenced by name, so evaluation order is always explicit and staged, never implicit. If the same KEY appears more than once in one attr_list, the last occurrence wins (all earlier occurrences of that key are discarded) — this is the sole, deterministic rule for duplicate/conflicting modifiers. An ACTION executes exactly once, at the point it is reached in sequential control flow (including when used as a CONDITION atom, per the CONDITION construct), producing a side effect and an optional return value.\n- [add] BIND: grammar `bind ::= IDENT "<-" (action | task_call)` — BIND is BrainCode\'s sole data-flow construct: it evaluates its right-hand side (an ACTION or a task_call) immediately and exactly once, at the point the BIND line is reached, then stores the resulting return value under IDENT as a local variable in the enclosing TASK body (or enclosing IF/FOREACH block — see those constructs for nested scoping). Variable names occupy their own namespace, disambiguated purely by shape: a bare IDENT with no following `[...]` or `(...)` is a variable reference; the same IDENT followed by `[...]` is an ACTION verb, and followed by `(...)` is a TASK call — so no name can be ambiguous between kinds. Within one TASK\'s top-level body, each variable name may be introduced by BIND at most once (single-assignment); referencing a variable before its BIND line, or re-BINDing the same name in the same scope, is a static error. Variables do not leak: they are visible only within the scope (TASK body, or nested IF/FOREACH block) in which they were bound, plus any nested blocks inside it.\n- [add] CONDITION: grammar `condition  ::= or_expr\nor_expr    ::= and_expr ("OR" and_expr)*\nand_expr   ::= not_expr ("AND" not_expr)*\nnot_expr   ::= ["NOT"] atom\natom       ::= comparison | action | "(" condition ")"\ncomparison ::= value comparator value\ncomparator ::= "==" | "!=" | "<" | ">" | "<=" | ">="` — CONDITION is a boolean expression built from a closed, fixed set of connectives (AND, OR, NOT) and comparators — no other logical operators exist. Precedence from tightest to loosest binding is NOT, then AND, then OR; all three are left-associative, and parentheses may override precedence explicitly. OR and AND short-circuit strictly left-to-right: the right operand of AND is not evaluated if the left is false, and the right operand of OR is not evaluated if the left is true — if that unevaluated operand is or contains an ACTION, that ACTION\'s side effect does not occur. An atom that is a `comparison` is pure (no side effect). An atom that is a bare `action` is explicitly effectful: it executes for its side effect at the moment it is evaluated, and its return value is coerced to boolean per that action\'s own contract (a declared boolean is used as-is; otherwise non-empty/non-zero/non-error results count as true, and empty/zero/error results count as false). Because an ACTION-as-atom is effectful, using the same check twice re-runs it twice; callers who need to reuse a result must BIND it once and reference the variable in the comparison instead.\n- [add] IF: grammar `if_stmt ::= "IF" condition ":" NEWLINE INDENT step+ DEDENT\n            ["ELSE" ":" NEWLINE INDENT step+ DEDENT]` — IF evaluates its CONDITION exactly once, applying CONDITION\'s own precedence/short-circuit rules. If the condition is true, the IF-block\'s steps execute in order, exactly as an unconditional sequence would; if false and an ELSE-block is present, its steps execute in order instead; the ELSE-block is optional and omitting it when the condition is false means no steps in this construct run at all. Steps inside the branch that is not taken never execute — this is the one place in the core basis where nested steps (including any BIND, ACTION, or nested TASK call) are conditionally skipped rather than unconditionally run; contrast with TASK bodies and FOREACH bodies, whose listed steps always run when that block is reached.\n- [add] FOREACH: grammar `foreach_stmt ::= "FOREACH" IDENT "IN" collection ":" NEWLINE INDENT step+ DEDENT\ncollection   ::= IDENT | list_literal | action\nlist_literal ::= "[" [value ("," value)*] "]"` — FOREACH evaluates its `collection` expression exactly once, at loop entry — if it is an ACTION, that action\'s side effect happens once and its returned sequence is what gets iterated; if it is a variable or list literal, that sequence is used directly. The loop variable IDENT is freshly bound at the start of each iteration, scoped only to that iteration\'s body (it shadows any outer variable of the same name for the duration of the loop and is not visible after the loop ends), which is an explicit exception to BIND\'s single-assignment-per-scope rule. Iterations run strictly in sequence, one at a time, in the collection\'s order — the core basis defines no concurrent or parallel iteration. Known gap, stated explicitly: BrainCode\'s core has no existential/\'at least one of\' or early-exit-on-first-match construct yet (e.g. \'try each search result until one works\'); FOREACH is exhaustive-sequential only, and any-of/parallel iteration is deferred to a future sprint.\n\nTask: "Enumeration Types are not usable in templates.\nDescription\n\t \n\t\t(last modified by Mariusz Felisiak)\n\t \nThe new \u200benumeration types are great but can\'t be used in Django templates due to their being callable. For example this doesn\'t work:\n{% if student.year_in_school == YearInSchool.FRESHMAN %}\nThis is because YearInSchool, being a class, is callable, and Django Templates always call callables with no arguments. The call fails because the required value argument is missing.\nThe easy solution would be to declare do_not_call_in_templates = True on the various Choices classes.\n"\n\nReturn JSON: {"braincode": "..."}'}], 'model': 'claude-sonnet-5', 'system': 'You are an independent translator for the BrainCode syntax. Given the current language spec, any proposed changes available for this translation, and one natural-language task, produce the BrainCode expression for the task. Use only constructs defined in the spec or the proposed changes; if something cannot be expressed, keep that part as a quoted natural-language string inside the expression rather than inventing syntax. Respond with only a JSON object: {"braincode": "<expression>"}'}}
