2026-09-19 11:40:20,337 [DEBUG] Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-9ea2427b-aec9-4d93-ad90-d7dca0f05a19', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are the **Critic** in the BrainCode syntax-creation loop (model role: OpenAI). You merge two\njobs that used to be separate roles: the adversarial **Reviewer** (logic and expressivity) and the\n**Examiner** (KPI assessment). You are not here to be agreeable, and you are not a statistical\nreferee — you are the one critical reader whose judgment decides whether this attempt\'s changes\nenter the language.\n\nBrainCode is a formal syntax for human agent-requests and agentic reasoning (task decomposition).\nIt is judged against five **pre-KPIs** — preliminary, directional goals, not measured metrics at\nthis phase:\n\n- **Coverage** — tasks from domains the language was *not* developed on can still be expressed\n  without falling back to natural language.\n- **Expressivity** — a BrainCode expression preserves what the natural-language request meant\n  (recipient, register, conditions, ordering, quantities, branches); nothing is lost round-trip.\n- **Determinism** — independent translators (different models, or repeated runs) converge on the\n  same or equivalent expression for the same task.\n- **Interpretability** — a reader with only the expression and the glossary can recover the\n  intent; the spec and glossary stay small, orthogonal, and unambiguous.\n- **Improvement** — a weaker model given the BrainCode expression would plan/act better than with\n  the raw request (structure that actually helps, not ceremony).\n\n## What you are shown\n\nThe Shaper\'s proposal — a `summary` and a list of `changes` (each `op: add | revise` with a\nconstruct\'s grammar, semantics, worked example, glossary gloss, change type, rationale) — plus the\ncurrent spec and glossary, a scripted doc-hygiene result, a random sample of dataset items\n(prompts and agent trajectories, e.g. Mind2Web web tasks, ALFRED household instructions, seed\ntasks), and, when available, independent translations of those items by other providers.\n\nThere may be several changes in one attempt. Judge them both individually and as a set: two\nchanges that are each fine can still overlap, conflict, or together make the grammar ambiguous.\n\n## What you must do\n\n1. **Break it.** For each change: find an input where the grammar has two valid readings or no\n   defined behavior; check the worked example actually matches the stated semantics; check the\n   gloss doesn\'t claim more than the semantics support; check for silent overlap with existing\n   constructs (redundancy hurts Determinism and Interpretability).\n2. **Simulate.** Translate **every** sampled item into BrainCode using the current spec plus the\n   proposed changes, exactly as a user of the language would. Put every one in `simulations` with\n   the actual expression you produced — never summarize them away. Score each `Full` (no\n   natural-language fallback), `Partial` (some part had to stay as prose), or `Fail`. Note in\n   `notes` which change helped, which was irrelevant, and what construct was missing.\n3. **Assess each pre-KPI.** For each of the five, decide whether this set of changes is a\n   `benefit`, `harm`, `neutral`, or `mixed` for that goal, and say why in terms of your\n   simulations and the logic you found. If independent translations were supplied, compare them\n   for Determinism (equivalent meaning despite surface differences counts as agreement); if not,\n   reason about how likely two different models would converge.\n4. **Say what would maximize benefit.** `required_changes` is the list of concrete edits the\n   Shaper must make — specific enough to act on (which construct, what to add/rename/define).\n   Include them even when you accept, if they would clearly improve the pre-KPIs further.\n5. **Decide.** `accept` when the net effect on the pre-KPIs is positive and nothing is logically\n   broken; `needs-rework` when the core idea is sound but specific issues must be fixed first;\n   `reject` only when the changes are fundamentally the wrong shape. Do not withhold acceptance for\n   lack of large-sample proof — there is no sample size, p-value, or threshold anywhere in this\n   process. Do not accept something ambiguous because it is "probably fine".\n\nIf the doc-hygiene line reports a failure, the attempt already cannot be accepted; still give the\nfull assessment so the Shaper\'s rework fixes everything at once.\n\n## Sprint 0 (bootstrap)\n\nWhen the proposal is the entire initial basis, additionally check, across the whole set:\ncross-construct consistency (no two constructs silently overlap or contradict each other);\nredundancy (two constructs covering the same ground); and the standard first-draft gaps —\nevaluation order of nested constructs; operator precedence/associativity/short-circuiting for any\nconnectives; pure vs. effectful conditions; identifier scoping and reference resolution\n(including recursion/cycles); data flow between steps; duplicate/conflicting modifiers; and\nnamed lexical primitives (identifiers, literals, whitespace, reserved words). A basis that leaves\nthese open costs every later sprint, so `needs-rework` with a precise `required_changes` list is\nthe right answer for a promising-but-underspecified basis.\n\n## Output\n\nRespond with **only** a JSON object, no prose outside it, matching exactly:\n\n```json\n{\n  "decision": "accept" | "needs-rework" | "reject",\n  "kpi_assessment": {\n    "coverage":         {"impact": "benefit" | "harm" | "neutral" | "mixed", "reasoning": "..."},\n    "expressivity":     {"impact": "...", "reasoning": "..."},\n    "determinism":      {"impact": "...", "reasoning": "..."},\n    "interpretability": {"impact": "...", "reasoning": "..."},\n    "improvement":      {"impact": "...", "reasoning": "..."}\n  },\n  "simulations": [\n    {"item_id": "...", "source": "...", "nl": "<the task>", "braincode": "<your expression>",\n     "coverage": "Full" | "Partial" | "Fail", "notes": "..."}\n  ],\n  "cross_check": {"used": true | false, "agreement": "high" | "partial" | "low" | "n/a", "notes": "..."},\n  "required_changes": [{"target": "<construct_name or \'basis\'>", "change": "<concrete edit>"}],\n  "logic_issues": ["<concrete issue>"],\n  "reasoning": "2-4 sentences justifying the decision in terms of the pre-KPIs"\n}\n```\n'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '# ROLE: critic_bootstrap\n\nThis is Sprint 0 (bootstrap): the proposal is the entire initial syntax basis, so judge it holistically — cross-construct consistency, redundancy, and the foundational-gap checklist.\n\nProposal (summary + all changes):\n{\n "foundations_overview": "BrainCode\'s core is a small set of orthogonal, composable constructs assembled into a context-free, indentation-delimited grammar (Python-style layout), so that structure — not free prose — carries meaning. Requests decompose into named TASKs containing a flat, top-to-bottom sequence of steps; nuance is pushed out of the verb vocabulary and into key=value attributes on atomic ACTIONs (HTML-style content/attribute separation); branching and iteration are closed constructs (IF/CONDITION/FOREACH) with fixed precedence and explicit evaluation order; and data flows between steps only through an explicit BIND, never through inline nesting of effects. Known gaps deliberately left for later sprints: no existential/\'any-of\' quantifier over collections, no parallel/concurrent execution, and no explicit error/exception-handling construct.",\n "inspiration_summary": "Python inspired the flat, indentation-delimited sequential block structure and single-assignment-style binding; HTML inspired separating a small closed set of ACTION verbs from open-ended key=value attributes; English inspired the closed-class logical connectives (AND/OR/NOT) in CONDITION; formal language theory and the Parsel/ReAct precedents inspired the context-free TASK-decomposition and effect-ordering rules that make the whole grammar deterministically parseable.",\n "summary": "Establishes a six-construct orthogonal core — TASK, ACTION, BIND, CONDITION, IF, FOREACH — covering decomposition, atomic effects, data flow, boolean logic, branching, and iteration, with explicit rules for evaluation order, precedence/short-circuiting, scoping, and duplicate-key handling.",\n "changes": [\n  {\n   "op": "add",\n   "construct_name": "TASK",\n   "grammar": "task_def   ::= \\"TASK\\" IDENT \\"(\\" [param_list] \\")\\" \\":\\" NEWLINE INDENT step+ DEDENT\\nparam_list ::= IDENT (\\",\\" IDENT)*\\nstep       ::= action | bind | if_stmt | foreach_stmt | task_call | return_stmt\\ntask_call  ::= IDENT \\"(\\" [arg_list] \\")\\"\\narg_list   ::= value (\\",\\" value)*\\nreturn_stmt::= \\"RETURN\\" value\\nvalue      ::= STRING | NUMBER | BOOLEAN | IDENT\\nIDENT      ::= /[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*/   (reserved words: TASK, IF, ELSE, FOREACH, IN, RETURN, AND, OR, NOT excluded)\\nSTRING     ::= \\"\\\\\\"\\" ... \\"\\\\\\"\\"   NUMBER ::= standard int/float literal   BOOLEAN ::= \\"true\\" | \\"false\\"\\ncomment    ::= \\"#\\" up-to-NEWLINE (ignored)",\n   "semantics": "TASK is the unit of decomposition and reuse: a name, a parameter list, and an ordered, indentation-delimited body of steps, all of which execute unconditionally and strictly top-to-bottom (Python-style flat sequencing — no implicit reordering or parallelism). Calling a TASK (task_call) fully evaluates its argument values first (arguments may only be STRING/NUMBER/BOOLEAN/IDENT — never an inline ACTION or another task_call; that must be BIND\'d first, see BIND), then executes the callee\'s body from its top, then control returns to the call site and resumes the next step. RETURN sets the TASK\'s return value and immediately ends that TASK\'s execution (no steps after RETURN in the same body run); a TASK with no RETURN returns no value. TASK names live in one global namespace, distinct from ACTION verbs and from local variable names (see BIND); a TASK may call itself or other TASKs, including mutual recursion — this is syntactically legal, cycles are not statically rejected, and non-termination is a runtime concern outside the grammar\'s scope. Lexical primitives (identifiers, string/number/boolean literals, `#`-to-end-of-line comments, and the reserved-word set above) are fixed and closed, so parsing never depends on surrounding context.",\n   "worked_example": {\n    "nl": "Plan a trip to a destination: book a flight, then book a hotel there, and give back the hotel confirmation.",\n    "braincode": "TASK PlanTrip(destination):\\n  flight <- BookFlight[to=destination]\\n  hotel <- BookHotel[city=destination]\\n  RETURN hotel"\n   },\n   "glossary_gloss": "TASK — a named, parameterized container holding an ordered, top-to-bottom sequence of steps; BrainCode\'s basic unit of decomposition, reuse, and (optionally recursive) composition.",\n   "change_type": "MAJOR",\n   "rationale": "Provides the flat, sequential, non-nested backbone the whole language composes from, directly serving Determinism and Interpretability per Python\'s \'small orthogonal core, avoid deep nesting\' lesson, while giving later sprints a place to hang decomposition-related constructs (dependency chains, sub-task calls) without redefining the basis.",\n   "cites_source": "python_notes; relevant_precedents (Parsel)"\n  },\n  {\n   "op": "add",\n   "construct_name": "ACTION",\n   "grammar": "action    ::= VERB \\"[\\" [attr_list] \\"]\\"\\nattr_list ::= attr (\\",\\" attr)*\\nattr      ::= KEY \\"=\\" value\\nVERB      ::= IDENT\\nKEY       ::= IDENT\\nvalue     ::= STRING | NUMBER | BOOLEAN | IDENT",\n   "semantics": "ACTION is the atomic, effectful primitive: a fixed-form VERB applied to zero or more key=value attributes that carry all situational nuance (recipient, tone, content, etc.), so the core verb vocabulary itself stays small (HTML-style content/attribute separation). Attribute values are restricted to literals or bare identifiers referring to already-BIND\'d variables (see BIND) — an ACTION may never appear inline as another ACTION\'s attribute value; a nested effect must first be captured with BIND and then referenced by name, so evaluation order is always explicit and staged, never implicit. If the same KEY appears more than once in one attr_list, the last occurrence wins (all earlier occurrences of that key are discarded) — this is the sole, deterministic rule for duplicate/conflicting modifiers. An ACTION executes exactly once, at the point it is reached in sequential control flow (including when used as a CONDITION atom, per the CONDITION construct), producing a side effect and an optional return value.",\n   "worked_example": {\n    "nl": "Email my mom asking about dinner plans, and cc my dad.",\n    "braincode": "Email[to=\\"mom\\", cc=\\"dad\\", body=\\"asking about dinner plans\\"]"\n   },\n   "glossary_gloss": "ACTION — an atomic, effectful primitive: a verb plus a set of key=value attributes supplying its nuance, with duplicate keys resolved last-write-wins and no inline nesting of other effects.",\n   "change_type": "MAJOR",\n   "rationale": "Directly implements HTML\'s content/attribute separation to keep the verb vocabulary tiny while attributes absorb variation (register, tone, recipient), maximizing Expressivity/Coverage without core bloat, and the no-inline-nesting rule pins down evaluation order per the Critic\'s checklist.",\n   "cites_source": "html_notes"\n  },\n  {\n   "op": "add",\n   "construct_name": "BIND",\n   "grammar": "bind ::= IDENT \\"<-\\" (action | task_call)",\n   "semantics": "BIND is BrainCode\'s sole data-flow construct: it evaluates its right-hand side (an ACTION or a task_call) immediately and exactly once, at the point the BIND line is reached, then stores the resulting return value under IDENT as a local variable in the enclosing TASK body (or enclosing IF/FOREACH block — see those constructs for nested scoping). Variable names occupy their own namespace, disambiguated purely by shape: a bare IDENT with no following `[...]` or `(...)` is a variable reference; the same IDENT followed by `[...]` is an ACTION verb, and followed by `(...)` is a TASK call — so no name can be ambiguous between kinds. Within one TASK\'s top-level body, each variable name may be introduced by BIND at most once (single-assignment); referencing a variable before its BIND line, or re-BINDing the same name in the same scope, is a static error. Variables do not leak: they are visible only within the scope (TASK body, or nested IF/FOREACH block) in which they were bound, plus any nested blocks inside it.",\n   "worked_example": {\n    "nl": "Check the weather in Tokyo and email it to Bob.",\n    "braincode": "TASK NotifyWeather():\\n  report <- GetWeather[city=\\"Tokyo\\"]\\n  Email[to=\\"Bob\\", body=report]"\n   },\n   "glossary_gloss": "BIND — captures the return value of an ACTION or TASK call under a single-assignment local variable name, the only mechanism by which one step\'s output feeds a later step\'s input.",\n   "change_type": "MAJOR",\n   "rationale": "Without an explicit binding construct, data flow between steps would have to be expressed via ambiguous inline nesting; BIND makes staging and reference resolution explicit and deterministic, closing the \'data flow / binding\' and \'identifier scoping\' gaps up front.",\n   "cites_source": "python_notes; relevant_precedents (Parsel, ReAct)"\n  },\n  {\n   "op": "add",\n   "construct_name": "CONDITION",\n   "grammar": "condition  ::= or_expr\\nor_expr    ::= and_expr (\\"OR\\" and_expr)*\\nand_expr   ::= not_expr (\\"AND\\" not_expr)*\\nnot_expr   ::= [\\"NOT\\"] atom\\natom       ::= comparison | action | \\"(\\" condition \\")\\"\\ncomparison ::= value comparator value\\ncomparator ::= \\"==\\" | \\"!=\\" | \\"<\\" | \\">\\" | \\"<=\\" | \\">=\\"",\n   "semantics": "CONDITION is a boolean expression built from a closed, fixed set of connectives (AND, OR, NOT) and comparators — no other logical operators exist. Precedence from tightest to loosest binding is NOT, then AND, then OR; all three are left-associative, and parentheses may override precedence explicitly. OR and AND short-circuit strictly left-to-right: the right operand of AND is not evaluated if the left is false, and the right operand of OR is not evaluated if the left is true — if that unevaluated operand is or contains an ACTION, that ACTION\'s side effect does not occur. An atom that is a `comparison` is pure (no side effect). An atom that is a bare `action` is explicitly effectful: it executes for its side effect at the moment it is evaluated, and its return value is coerced to boolean per that action\'s own contract (a declared boolean is used as-is; otherwise non-empty/non-zero/non-error results count as true, and empty/zero/error results count as false). Because an ACTION-as-atom is effectful, using the same check twice re-runs it twice; callers who need to reuse a result must BIND it once and reference the variable in the comparison instead.",\n   "worked_example": {\n    "nl": "Notify Bob if the flight status is delayed and he hasn\'t already been notified.",\n    "braincode": "status <- FlightStatus[id=flightId]\\nIF status == \\"delayed\\" AND NOT notified:\\n  Email[to=\\"Bob\\", body=\\"Your flight is delayed.\\"]"\n   },\n   "glossary_gloss": "CONDITION — a boolean expression over a closed set of connectives (AND, OR, NOT) and comparators, with fixed precedence NOT > AND > OR, left-to-right short-circuit evaluation, and atoms that may be pure comparisons or explicitly effectful ACTION-checks.",\n   "change_type": "MAJOR",\n   "rationale": "English\'s closed-class function words justify bounding the logical vocabulary tightly; giving explicit precedence, associativity, and short-circuit rules, plus an explicit pure-vs-effectful statement for action-as-check, closes two items the Critic checks for directly.",\n   "cites_source": "english_notes"\n  },\n  {\n   "op": "add",\n   "construct_name": "IF",\n   "grammar": "if_stmt ::= \\"IF\\" condition \\":\\" NEWLINE INDENT step+ DEDENT\\n            [\\"ELSE\\" \\":\\" NEWLINE INDENT step+ DEDENT]",\n   "semantics": "IF evaluates its CONDITION exactly once, applying CONDITION\'s own precedence/short-circuit rules. If the condition is true, the IF-block\'s steps execute in order, exactly as an unconditional sequence would; if false and an ELSE-block is present, its steps execute in order instead; the ELSE-block is optional and omitting it when the condition is false means no steps in this construct run at all. Steps inside the branch that is not taken never execute — this is the one place in the core basis where nested steps (including any BIND, ACTION, or nested TASK call) are conditionally skipped rather than unconditionally run; contrast with TASK bodies and FOREACH bodies, whose listed steps always run when that block is reached.",\n   "worked_example": {\n    "nl": "Check if the store is open; if so, order groceries, otherwise remind me tomorrow.",\n    "braincode": "open <- CheckStoreHours[store=\\"Corner Market\\"]\\nIF open == true:\\n  OrderGroceries[store=\\"Corner Market\\", items=shoppingList]\\nELSE:\\n  Remind[when=\\"tomorrow\\", message=\\"Order groceries\\"]"\n   },\n   "glossary_gloss": "IF — branches on a CONDITION, running exactly one of an IF-block or optional ELSE-block; the untaken branch\'s steps and effects never execute.",\n   "change_type": "MAJOR",\n   "rationale": "Conditional branching is a minimal, orthogonal addition on top of CONDITION and step-sequences already defined by TASK, and explicitly states which nested steps evaluate and when, closing the \'evaluation order\' and \'pure vs effectful\' checklist items for branchy real-world requests.",\n   "cites_source": "english_notes; relevant_precedents (ReAct)"\n  },\n  {\n   "op": "add",\n   "construct_name": "FOREACH",\n   "grammar": "foreach_stmt ::= \\"FOREACH\\" IDENT \\"IN\\" collection \\":\\" NEWLINE INDENT step+ DEDENT\\ncollection   ::= IDENT | list_literal | action\\nlist_literal ::= \\"[\\" [value (\\",\\" value)*] \\"]\\"",\n   "semantics": "FOREACH evaluates its `collection` expression exactly once, at loop entry — if it is an ACTION, that action\'s side effect happens once and its returned sequence is what gets iterated; if it is a variable or list literal, that sequence is used directly. The loop variable IDENT is freshly bound at the start of each iteration, scoped only to that iteration\'s body (it shadows any outer variable of the same name for the duration of the loop and is not visible after the loop ends), which is an explicit exception to BIND\'s single-assignment-per-scope rule. Iterations run strictly in sequence, one at a time, in the collection\'s order — the core basis defines no concurrent or parallel iteration. Known gap, stated explicitly: BrainCode\'s core has no existential/\'at least one of\' or early-exit-on-first-match construct yet (e.g. \'try each search result until one works\'); FOREACH is exhaustive-sequential only, and any-of/parallel iteration is deferred to a future sprint.",\n   "worked_example": {\n    "nl": "Search each of these three websites for the cheapest flight to Rome and log the price found on each.",\n    "braincode": "FOREACH site IN [\\"site-a.com\\", \\"site-b.com\\", \\"site-c.com\\"]:\\n  price <- SearchFlight[site=site, destination=\\"Rome\\"]\\n  LogResult[site=site, price=price]"\n   },\n   "glossary_gloss": "FOREACH — iterates a loop body once per element of a collection (evaluated once, in fixed order), sequentially and exhaustively, with a fresh loop-scoped binding each iteration.",\n   "change_type": "MAJOR",\n   "rationale": "Real agent tasks routinely range over lists of stops/files/results, so a minimal iteration primitive is required in the initial basis; explicitly naming the missing existential/parallel case here (rather than leaving it implicit) satisfies the Critic\'s iteration/collections checklist item.",\n   "cites_source": "python_notes"\n  }\n ],\n "_provider_used": "anthropic",\n "_model_used": "claude-sonnet-5"\n}\n\nCurrent language-spec.md:\n# BrainCode Language Specification\n\n**Version:** 0.1.0\n**Status:** not yet bootstrapped — Sprint 0 will run automatically on the next invocation against this empty spec.\n\n## Purpose\n\nBrainCode is a formal syntax for expressing human agent-requests and agentic reasoning\n(task decomposition) that is:\n\n- **Expressive** — captures what natural language captures (negation, quantifiers, conditionals,\n  temporal ordering, causal chains, multi-step dependency) without redundancy.\n- **Broad-coverage** — generalizes to domains not seen during its own development.\n- **Deterministic** — independent translators (different models, or repeated runs) converge on\n  the same or equivalent expression for the same task.\n- **Interpretable** — recoverable to plain-language intent by a human or LLM reader, given only\n  the expression and this documentation.\n- **Style-independent** — does not encode the linguistic idiosyncrasies of whichever model wrote\n  it (e.g. "email my mom" vs. "email my professor" should differ in *content* the syntax\n  captures — recipient, register — not in incidental phrasing).\n\nThis document is the frozen reference during any evaluation. Do not hand-edit it outside of a\nsprint — use the orchestrator so `docs/changelog.md` and `docs/glossary.md` stay in sync.\n\n## Foundations\n\n*(To be filled in by Sprint 0 — the Searcher gathers structural notes on the inspiration\nlanguages configured in `config.yaml -> run.bootstrap.inspiration_languages`, then the Shaper\nproposes the initial basis.)*\n\n## Constructs\n\n*(No constructs accepted yet. Each accepted change appends or rewrites one section here: name,\ngrammar, semantics, one worked example, and a pointer to its `glossary.md` entry and the\nsprint/changelog entry that introduced or last revised it.)*\n\n\nCurrent glossary.md:\n# BrainCode Operator Glossary\n\nOne entry per construct in `language-spec.md`. Every entry has: a plain-language gloss (one or\ntwo sentences, no jargon) and one worked example (NL → BrainCode). This file is what the\nscripted doc-hygiene check enforces every attempt — a change with no gloss or no example is sent\nback before the Critic even reads it (`braincode_loop/doc_hygiene.py`).\n\n*(Empty — no constructs accepted yet.)*\n\n<!-- Format for each entry, written by the Documenter role (rewritten in place on `revise`):\n\n### `<operator-name>`\n\n**Gloss:** plain-language explanation.\n\n**Example:**\n- NL: "..."\n- BrainCode: `...`\n\n**Introduced/last revised:** sprint N, see `changelog.md#sprint-N`\n\n-->\n\n\nDoc-hygiene check (scripted, structural): All changes carry a complete glossary entry (gloss + worked example).\n\nSampled dataset items to simulate (4):\n[\n {\n  "item_id": "dev-code-1",\n  "source": "seed_tasks",\n  "domain": "code_task (dev)",\n  "nl": "Given a list of file paths, delete every file older than 30 days unless it\'s in the \'archive\' folder."\n },\n {\n  "item_id": "8f567f79-e197-4d7e-9a49-877daae6dde5",\n  "source": "Mind2Web (osunlp/Mind2Web, train, Parquet column-projected sample)",\n  "domain": "Shopping",\n  "nl": "Find a Blue iPhone 12 Pro 128gb and add to cart.",\n  "trajectory_steps": [\n   "[combobox]  Search for anything -> TYPE: iPhone 12 Pro",\n   "[button]  Search -> CLICK",\n   "[link]  128 GB - apply Storage Capacity filter -> CLICK",\n   "[heading]  Apple iPhone 12 Pro - 128GB - All Colors - Unlocke... -> CLICK",\n   "[combobox]  Please select a Color -> SELECT: Blue",\n   "[link]  Add to cart -> CLICK"\n  ]\n },\n {\n  "item_id": "trial_T20190907_193025_428000#1",\n  "source": "ALFRED (json_2.1.0, train)",\n  "domain": "pick_heat_then_place_in_recep",\n  "nl": "Put a warmed tomato into the fridge.",\n  "trajectory_steps": [\n   "Go forward and then to the left to face the sink.",\n   "Pick the tomato up from the sink.",\n   "Go right and turn to face the microwave.",\n   "Put the tomato into the microwave and turn it on and then open the door and take the tomato out and shut the door.",\n   "Turn around and cross the room and then turn to the right to face the fridge.",\n   "Put the tomato into the fridge and shut the door to the fridge."\n  ]\n },\n {\n  "item_id": "django__django-12304",\n  "source": "SWE-bench_Verified (princeton-nlp/SWE-bench_Verified, test, Parquet column-projected sample)",\n  "domain": "django/django",\n  "nl": "Enumeration Types are not usable in templates.\\nDescription\\n\\t \\n\\t\\t(last modified by Mariusz Felisiak)\\n\\t \\nThe new \u200benumeration types are great but can\'t be used in Django templates due to their being callable. For example this doesn\'t work:\\n{% if student.year_in_school == YearInSchool.FRESHMAN %}\\nThis is because YearInSchool, being a class, is callable, and Django Templates always call callables with no arguments. The call fails because the required value argument is missing.\\nThe easy solution would be to declare do_not_call_in_templates = True on the various Choices classes.\\n",\n  "trajectory_steps": [\n   "modify django/db/models/enums.py"\n  ]\n }\n]\n\nIndependent translations of the sampled items by other providers (evidence for the Determinism pre-KPI — compare them to each other and to your own simulation):\n{\n "dev-code-1": {\n  "anthropic": "TASK CleanOldFiles(file_paths):\\n    FOREACH path IN file_paths:\\n        age <- GET_FILE_AGE_DAYS[path=path]\\n        in_archive <- IS_IN_FOLDER[path=path, folder=\\"archive\\"]\\n        IF age > 30 AND NOT in_archive:\\n            DELETE_FILE[path=path]",\n  "gemini": "TASK DeleteOldFiles(file_paths):\\n    FOREACH path IN file_paths:\\n        IF NOT IsInFolder[path=path, folder=\\"archive\\"] AND IsOlderThan[path=path, days=30]:\\n            DeleteFile[path=path]"\n },\n "8f567f79-e197-4d7e-9a49-877daae6dde5": {\n  "anthropic": "TASK FindAndAddToCart():\\n    item <- SEARCH[product=\\"iPhone 12 Pro\\", color=\\"Blue\\", storage=\\"128GB\\"]\\n    ADD_TO_CART[item=item]",\n  "gemini": "TASK main():\\n    iphone <- find[query=\\"Blue iPhone 12 Pro 128gb\\"]\\n    add_to_cart[item=iphone]"\n },\n "trial_T20190907_193025_428000#1": {\n  "anthropic": "TASK PutWarmedTomatoInFridge():\\n    tomato <- WARM[item=\\"tomato\\"]\\n    PUT[item=tomato, destination=\\"fridge\\"]",\n  "gemini": "TASK Main():\\n    tomato <- Get[item=\\"tomato\\"]\\n    Warm[item=tomato]\\n    Put[item=tomato, destination=\\"fridge\\"]"\n },\n "django__django-12304": {\n  "anthropic": "TASK FixEnumTemplateUsage():\\n    # Enumeration types like YearInSchool are callable classes; Django Templates call callables with no arguments, so `{% if student.year_in_school == YearInSchool.FRESHMAN %}` fails because the required value argument is missing.\\n    SET_ATTRIBUTE[target=\\"Choices classes\\", key=\\"do_not_call_in_templates\\", value=true]",\n  "gemini": "TASK FixDjangoEnumTemplates():\\n    choices_classes <- Find[target=\\"Choices classes\\"]\\n    FOREACH cls IN choices_classes:\\n        Declare[target=cls, attribute=\\"do_not_call_in_templates\\", value=true]"\n }\n}\n\nReturn only the JSON object specified in your instructions; include one `simulations` entry per sampled item.'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.6-terra', 'max_completion_tokens': 20000}}
