2026-09-19 11:41:25,975 [DEBUG] Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/v1/messages', 'headers': {'anthropic-user-profile-id': <anthropic.Omit object at 0x000002C9C7F61050>}, 'timeout': Timeout(connect=5.0, read=600, write=600, pool=600), 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-b3df4225-7142-496a-b1eb-95e24e0b2138', 'content': None, 'json_data': {'max_tokens': 20000, 'messages': [{'role': 'user', 'content': '# ROLE: shaper_bootstrap\n\nThis is Sprint 0 (bootstrap): the language has no constructs yet. Propose an initial syntax **basis** — a small set of foundational, composable constructs (aim for 3-6, every change `op: "add"`, change_type MAJOR) that together can express simple agent-requests, drawing structural inspiration from: python, html, english. Prefer a small orthogonal core over redundant coverage — every later sprint composes from these.\n\nYOUR PREVIOUS ATTEMPT WAS SENT BACK — this is a revision, not a fresh start.\nPrevious changes: [{"op": "add", "construct_name": "TASK", "grammar": "task_def   ::= \\"TASK\\" IDENT \\"(\\" [param_list] \\")\\" \\":\\" NEWLINE INDENT step+ DEDENT\\nparam_list ::= IDENT (\\",\\" IDENT)*\\nstep       ::= action | bind | if_stmt | foreach_stmt | task_call | return_stmt\\ntask_call  ::= IDENT \\"(\\" [arg_list] \\")\\"\\narg_list   ::= value (\\",\\" value)*\\nreturn_stmt::= \\"RETURN\\" value\\nvalue      ::= STRING | NUMBER | BOOLEAN | IDENT\\nIDENT      ::= /[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*/   (reserved words: TASK, IF, ELSE, FOREACH, IN, RETURN, AND, OR, NOT excluded)\\nSTRING     ::= \\"\\\\\\"\\" ... \\"\\\\\\"\\"   NUMBER ::= standard int/float literal   BOOLEAN ::= \\"true\\" | \\"false\\"\\ncomment    ::= \\"#\\" up-to-NEWLINE (ignored)", "semantics": "TASK is the unit of decomposition and reuse: a name, a parameter list, and an ordered, indentation-delimited body of steps, all of which execute unconditionally and strictly top-to-bottom (Python-style flat sequencing — no implicit reordering or parallelism). Calling a TASK (task_call) fully evaluates its argument values first (arguments may only be STRING/NUMBER/BOOLEAN/IDENT — never an inline ACTION or another task_call; that must be BIND\'d first, see BIND), then executes the callee\'s body from its top, then control returns to the call site and resumes the next step. RETURN sets the TASK\'s return value and immediately ends that TASK\'s execution (no steps after RETURN in the same body run); a TASK with no RETURN returns no value. TASK names live in one global namespace, distinct from ACTION verbs and from local variable names (see BIND); a TASK may call itself or other TASKs, including mutual recursion — this is syntactically legal, cycles are not statically rejected, and non-termination is a runtime concern outside the grammar\'s scope. Lexical primitives (identifiers, string/number/boolean literals, `#`-to-end-of-line comments, and the reserved-word set above) are fixed and closed, so parsing never depends on surrounding context.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Plan a trip to a destination: book a flight, then book a hotel there, and give back the hotel confirmation.", "braincode": "TASK PlanTrip(destination):\\n  flight <- BookFlight[to=destination]\\n  hotel <- BookHotel[city=destination]\\n  RETURN hotel"}, "glossary_gloss": "TASK — a named, parameterized container holding an ordered, top-to-bottom sequence of steps; BrainCode\'s basic unit of decomposition, reuse, and (optionally recursive) composition.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Provides the flat, sequential, non-nested backbone the whole language composes from, directly serving Determinism and Interpretability per Python\'s \'small orthogonal core, avoid deep nesting\' lesson, while giving later sprints a place to hang decomposition-related constructs (dependency chains, sub-task calls) without redefining the basis.", "cites_source": "python_notes; relevant_precedents (Parsel)"}, {"op": "add", "construct_name": "ACTION", "grammar": "action    ::= VERB \\"[\\" [attr_list] \\"]\\"\\nattr_list ::= attr (\\",\\" attr)*\\nattr      ::= KEY \\"=\\" value\\nVERB      ::= IDENT\\nKEY       ::= IDENT\\nvalue     ::= STRING | NUMBER | BOOLEAN | IDENT", "semantics": "ACTION is the atomic, effectful primitive: a fixed-form VERB applied to zero or more key=value attributes that carry all situational nuance (recipient, tone, content, etc.), so the core verb vocabulary itself stays small (HTML-style content/attribute separation). Attribute values are restricted to literals or bare identifiers referring to already-BIND\'d variables (see BIND) — an ACTION may never appear inline as another ACTION\'s attribute value; a nested effect must first be captured with BIND and then referenced by name, so evaluation order is always explicit and staged, never implicit. If the same KEY appears more than once in one attr_list, the last occurrence wins (all earlier occurrences of that key are discarded) — this is the sole, deterministic rule for duplicate/conflicting modifiers. An ACTION executes exactly once, at the point it is reached in sequential control flow (including when used as a CONDITION atom, per the CONDITION construct), producing a side effect and an optional return value.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Email my mom asking about dinner plans, and cc my dad.", "braincode": "Email[to=\\"mom\\", cc=\\"dad\\", body=\\"asking about dinner plans\\"]"}, "glossary_gloss": "ACTION — an atomic, effectful primitive: a verb plus a set of key=value attributes supplying its nuance, with duplicate keys resolved last-write-wins and no inline nesting of other effects.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Directly implements HTML\'s content/attribute separation to keep the verb vocabulary tiny while attributes absorb variation (register, tone, recipient), maximizing Expressivity/Coverage without core bloat, and the no-inline-nesting rule pins down evaluation order per the Critic\'s checklist.", "cites_source": "html_notes"}, {"op": "add", "construct_name": "BIND", "grammar": "bind ::= IDENT \\"<-\\" (action | task_call)", "semantics": "BIND is BrainCode\'s sole data-flow construct: it evaluates its right-hand side (an ACTION or a task_call) immediately and exactly once, at the point the BIND line is reached, then stores the resulting return value under IDENT as a local variable in the enclosing TASK body (or enclosing IF/FOREACH block — see those constructs for nested scoping). Variable names occupy their own namespace, disambiguated purely by shape: a bare IDENT with no following `[...]` or `(...)` is a variable reference; the same IDENT followed by `[...]` is an ACTION verb, and followed by `(...)` is a TASK call — so no name can be ambiguous between kinds. Within one TASK\'s top-level body, each variable name may be introduced by BIND at most once (single-assignment); referencing a variable before its BIND line, or re-BINDing the same name in the same scope, is a static error. Variables do not leak: they are visible only within the scope (TASK body, or nested IF/FOREACH block) in which they were bound, plus any nested blocks inside it.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Check the weather in Tokyo and email it to Bob.", "braincode": "TASK NotifyWeather():\\n  report <- GetWeather[city=\\"Tokyo\\"]\\n  Email[to=\\"Bob\\", body=report]"}, "glossary_gloss": "BIND — captures the return value of an ACTION or TASK call under a single-assignment local variable name, the only mechanism by which one step\'s output feeds a later step\'s input.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Without an explicit binding construct, data flow between steps would have to be expressed via ambiguous inline nesting; BIND makes staging and reference resolution explicit and deterministic, closing the \'data flow / binding\' and \'identifier scoping\' gaps up front.", "cites_source": "python_notes; relevant_precedents (Parsel, ReAct)"}, {"op": "add", "construct_name": "CONDITION", "grammar": "condition  ::= or_expr\\nor_expr    ::= and_expr (\\"OR\\" and_expr)*\\nand_expr   ::= not_expr (\\"AND\\" not_expr)*\\nnot_expr   ::= [\\"NOT\\"] atom\\natom       ::= comparison | action | \\"(\\" condition \\")\\"\\ncomparison ::= value comparator value\\ncomparator ::= \\"==\\" | \\"!=\\" | \\"<\\" | \\">\\" | \\"<=\\" | \\">=\\"", "semantics": "CONDITION is a boolean expression built from a closed, fixed set of connectives (AND, OR, NOT) and comparators — no other logical operators exist. Precedence from tightest to loosest binding is NOT, then AND, then OR; all three are left-associative, and parentheses may override precedence explicitly. OR and AND short-circuit strictly left-to-right: the right operand of AND is not evaluated if the left is false, and the right operand of OR is not evaluated if the left is true — if that unevaluated operand is or contains an ACTION, that ACTION\'s side effect does not occur. An atom that is a `comparison` is pure (no side effect). An atom that is a bare `action` is explicitly effectful: it executes for its side effect at the moment it is evaluated, and its return value is coerced to boolean per that action\'s own contract (a declared boolean is used as-is; otherwise non-empty/non-zero/non-error results count as true, and empty/zero/error results count as false). Because an ACTION-as-atom is effectful, using the same check twice re-runs it twice; callers who need to reuse a result must BIND it once and reference the variable in the comparison instead.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Notify Bob if the flight status is delayed and he hasn\'t already been notified.", "braincode": "status <- FlightStatus[id=flightId]\\nIF status == \\"delayed\\" AND NOT notified:\\n  Email[to=\\"Bob\\", body=\\"Your flight is delayed.\\"]"}, "glossary_gloss": "CONDITION — a boolean expression over a closed set of connectives (AND, OR, NOT) and comparators, with fixed precedence NOT > AND > OR, left-to-right short-circuit evaluation, and atoms that may be pure comparisons or explicitly effectful ACTION-checks.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "English\'s closed-class function words justify bounding the logical vocabulary tightly; giving explicit precedence, associativity, and short-circuit rules, plus an explicit pure-vs-effectful statement for action-as-check, closes two items the Critic checks for directly.", "cites_source": "english_notes"}, {"op": "add", "construct_name": "IF", "grammar": "if_stmt ::= \\"IF\\" condition \\":\\" NEWLINE INDENT step+ DEDENT\\n            [\\"ELSE\\" \\":\\" NEWLINE INDENT step+ DEDENT]", "semantics": "IF evaluates its CONDITION exactly once, applying CONDITION\'s own precedence/short-circuit rules. If the condition is true, the IF-block\'s steps execute in order, exactly as an unconditional sequence would; if false and an ELSE-block is present, its steps execute in order instead; the ELSE-block is optional and omitting it when the condition is false means no steps in this construct run at all. Steps inside the branch that is not taken never execute — this is the one place in the core basis where nested steps (including any BIND, ACTION, or nested TASK call) are conditionally skipped rather than unconditionally run; contrast with TASK bodies and FOREACH bodies, whose listed steps always run when that block is reached.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Check if the store is open; if so, order groceries, otherwise remind me tomorrow.", "braincode": "open <- CheckStoreHours[store=\\"Corner Market\\"]\\nIF open == true:\\n  OrderGroceries[store=\\"Corner Market\\", items=shoppingList]\\nELSE:\\n  Remind[when=\\"tomorrow\\", message=\\"Order groceries\\"]"}, "glossary_gloss": "IF — branches on a CONDITION, running exactly one of an IF-block or optional ELSE-block; the untaken branch\'s steps and effects never execute.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Conditional branching is a minimal, orthogonal addition on top of CONDITION and step-sequences already defined by TASK, and explicitly states which nested steps evaluate and when, closing the \'evaluation order\' and \'pure vs effectful\' checklist items for branchy real-world requests.", "cites_source": "english_notes; relevant_precedents (ReAct)"}, {"op": "add", "construct_name": "FOREACH", "grammar": "foreach_stmt ::= \\"FOREACH\\" IDENT \\"IN\\" collection \\":\\" NEWLINE INDENT step+ DEDENT\\ncollection   ::= IDENT | list_literal | action\\nlist_literal ::= \\"[\\" [value (\\",\\" value)*] \\"]\\"", "semantics": "FOREACH evaluates its `collection` expression exactly once, at loop entry — if it is an ACTION, that action\'s side effect happens once and its returned sequence is what gets iterated; if it is a variable or list literal, that sequence is used directly. The loop variable IDENT is freshly bound at the start of each iteration, scoped only to that iteration\'s body (it shadows any outer variable of the same name for the duration of the loop and is not visible after the loop ends), which is an explicit exception to BIND\'s single-assignment-per-scope rule. Iterations run strictly in sequence, one at a time, in the collection\'s order — the core basis defines no concurrent or parallel iteration. Known gap, stated explicitly: BrainCode\'s core has no existential/\'at least one of\' or early-exit-on-first-match construct yet (e.g. \'try each search result until one works\'); FOREACH is exhaustive-sequential only, and any-of/parallel iteration is deferred to a future sprint.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Search each of these three websites for the cheapest flight to Rome and log the price found on each.", "braincode": "FOREACH site IN [\\"site-a.com\\", \\"site-b.com\\", \\"site-c.com\\"]:\\n  price <- SearchFlight[site=site, destination=\\"Rome\\"]\\n  LogResult[site=site, price=price]"}, "glossary_gloss": "FOREACH — iterates a loop body once per element of a collection (evaluated once, in fixed order), sequentially and exhaustively, with a fresh loop-scoped binding each iteration.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Real agent tasks routinely range over lists of stops/files/results, so a minimal iteration primitive is required in the initial basis; explicitly naming the missing existential/parallel case here (rather than leaving it implicit) satisfies the Critic\'s iteration/collections checklist item.", "cites_source": "python_notes"}]\nCritic\'s decision: needs-rework\nCritic\'s reasoning: This is a promising structural core: it provides explicit sequencing, binding, branching, and iteration, and all sampled requests can be rendered without prose fallback. It cannot be accepted as a bootstrap basis because its own examples are often outside its grammar, loop scoping contradicts single assignment, and the unconstrained ACTION layer makes independently translated meaning non-deterministic and not recoverable from the specification. The required edits are foundational rather than cosmetic, but they preserve the core design rather than requiring its rejection.\nRequired changes (address every one explicitly): [{"target": "basis", "change": "Define a complete program entry grammar: state whether a BrainCode document is one or more TASK definitions, how a request identifies its entry TASK, and prohibit or define top-level ACTION, BIND, IF, and FOREACH forms. Revise every worked example to be a valid complete document under that grammar."}, {"target": "ACTION", "change": "Resolve the contradiction between a purported small/fixed verb vocabulary and `VERB ::= IDENT`. Either define a closed, glossed action vocabulary with canonical required/optional attributes, or explicitly make actions externally declared and add a declaration/registry mechanism that specifies each verb\'s effects, return type, boolean behavior, and canonical attribute names."}, {"target": "BIND", "change": "Define parameter bindings as initial local bindings, define whether TASK names may collide with local variables or action names despite shape-based parsing, and define scope activation for repeated FOREACH iterations. Explicitly state that BINDs in a loop body are fresh per iteration, or otherwise change the grammar/semantics so the demonstrated loop programs are legal."}, {"target": "IF", "change": "Define a branch-result/data-flow rule: either provide a join binding construct or explicitly specify how a value conditionally produced in IF/ELSE can be used after the construct. Also define the static validity of same-named BINDs in opposite branch scopes."}, {"target": "CONDITION", "change": "Specify comparison typing and behavior for mismatched types, ordering of strings and booleans, undefined values, and action errors. Replace the circular phrase \'per that action\'s own contract\' with either a uniform ACTION return/boolean contract or required declared contracts."}, {"target": "TASK", "change": "Complete lexical and layout rules: give STRING escaping and newline rules, NUMBER syntax, whitespace/indentation and tab rules, comment placement, newline termination, and the lexer rule distinguishing DEDENT followed by ELSE. Define duplicate parameter handling and argument-count/type errors."}, {"target": "FOREACH", "change": "Make collection values part of the common value grammar if they are intended to be passed to TASKs or ACTIONs, and define collection type errors and behavior for null/non-sequence ACTION results. Reconcile the statement that BIND is the sole data-flow mechanism with direct ACTION evaluation in a FOREACH collection position."}, {"target": "worked examples", "change": "Correct invalid examples: the CONDITION example references unbound flightId and notified and is not enclosed in a TASK; the IF and FOREACH examples are likewise top-level despite the proposed grammar only defining TASK documents. Ensure all example identifiers are parameters, literals, or earlier legal bindings."}]\nLogic issues: ["The proposed top-level grammar defines TASK as the only complete definition but several worked examples consist of BIND, IF, or FOREACH statements at top level; those examples are not derivable from the stated grammar.", "FOREACH says its loop binding is fresh per iteration, but BIND permits each name only once in a scope and does not say that each loop iteration creates a fresh body scope. The provided FOREACH example consequently re-BINDs price on iteration two under one valid reading.", "ACTION claims a small fixed verb vocabulary, while `VERB ::= IDENT` permits every non-reserved identifier and no vocabulary or action declaration is supplied.", "ACTION boolean coercion depends on an action-specific contract, but the language defines no location or syntax for such contracts. Therefore an effectful CONDITION atom has no recoverable truth semantics.", "TASK parameter names are used as variable references but are not defined as bindings, and parameter scope, duplicate parameters, argument arity, and type behavior are unspecified.", "The grammar states that NOT is left-associative even though it is unary and only permits a single optional NOT; `NOT NOT x` has no parse although repeated negation is normally implied by the connective set.", "The common value grammar excludes list literals, so lists can only appear as FOREACH collections and cannot be supplied to actions or task calls despite list processing being a core use case.", "The claimed sole-data-flow rule for BIND is contradicted by FOREACH accepting an ACTION directly as a collection expression and consuming its return value without a BIND.", "No semantics distinguish source-code editing from runtime actions, nor define action return values and mutation behavior; consequently the Django and ALFRED plans are syntactically legal but operationally underdetermined."]\nPre-KPI assessment: {"coverage": {"impact": "benefit", "reasoning": "The six constructs can structurally encode all four sampled tasks, including file iteration, web search and selection, physical-object manipulation, and a source-code change. However, coverage is constrained by the absence of structured values beyond a loop-only list literal and by undefined ACTION contracts, so many domains would require inventing incompatible verb and attribute conventions."}, "expressivity": {"impact": "mixed", "reasoning": "Sequencing, dependencies, conditional exclusion, and exhaustive iteration are expressed directly in the simulations. But the basis cannot pass a list as an ACTION or TASK argument, cannot reliably express values produced in mutually exclusive branches for later use, and leaves comparison type behavior and arbitrary ACTION return contracts undefined."}, "determinism": {"impact": "harm", "reasoning": "The grammar parses many structural forms deterministically, but translation is not deterministic because VERB and KEY are unrestricted identifiers with no canonical action or attribute vocabulary. The independent translations demonstrate this: they disagree on whether checking age is a separate BIND or an effectful condition, whether warming requires acquiring the tomato first, and whether the Django fix targets a generic class collection or named Choices classes."}, "interpretability": {"impact": "mixed", "reasoning": "Explicit BIND, branch blocks, loop order, and short-circuit rules are readable and useful. Conversely, expressions such as Search, Find, GET_FILE_AGE_DAYS, IsOlderThan, SetAttribute, and Declare have no declared semantics, so a reader cannot recover their operational meaning from the glossary alone; the claim that ACTION uses a small fixed verb vocabulary also contradicts its IDENT grammar."}, "improvement": {"impact": "benefit", "reasoning": "The simulations expose useful plans that raw requests leave implicit: the file-cleanup guard, the dependency from search result to cart insertion, and the intermediate tomato reference. This benefit is weakened by unresolved scope behavior in repeated loop bodies and by arbitrary action naming, but the structural core would still usually help a weaker planner."}}\nSimulated examples that exposed gaps: [{"item_id": "dev-code-1", "source": "seed_tasks", "nl": "Given a list of file paths, delete every file older than 30 days unless it\'s in the \'archive\' folder.", "braincode": "TASK CleanOldFiles(file_paths):\\n  FOREACH path IN file_paths:\\n    age <- GetFileAgeDays[path=path]\\n    archived <- IsInFolder[path=path, folder=\\"archive\\"]\\n    IF age > 30 AND archived == false:\\n      DeleteFile[path=path]", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "FOREACH, BIND, and IF directly express exhaustive processing and the unless condition. This relies on the intended-but-underspecified interpretation that BIND names in a loop body are fresh for every iteration; under the written single-assignment rule, later iterations re-BIND age and archived."}, {"item_id": "8f567f79-e197-4d7e-9a49-877daae6dde5", "source": "Mind2Web (osunlp/Mind2Web, train, Parquet column-projected sample)", "nl": "Find a Blue iPhone 12 Pro 128gb and add to cart.", "braincode": "TASK FindAndAddToCart():\\n  item <- Search[product=\\"iPhone 12 Pro\\", color=\\"Blue\\", storage=\\"128GB\\"]\\n  AddToCart[item=item]", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "BIND makes the search-result-to-cart dependency explicit. ACTION supplies no canonical distinction between a search query, applying a filter, selecting a variant, and a product lookup, so other translators can validly produce materially different plans."}, {"item_id": "trial_T20190907_193025_428000#1", "source": "ALFRED (json_2.1.0, train)", "nl": "Put a warmed tomato into the fridge.", "braincode": "TASK PutWarmedTomatoInFridge():\\n  tomato <- Pick[item=\\"tomato\\"]\\n  Warm[item=tomato]\\n  Put[item=tomato, destination=\\"fridge\\"]", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "TASK sequencing and BIND express acquisition before warming and placement. The proposal offers no ACTION-level semantics for whether Warm changes the referenced object in place, returns a new object, or must include appliance-opening and closing operations."}, {"item_id": "django__django-12304", "source": "SWE-bench_Verified (princeton-nlp/SWE-bench_Verified, test, Parquet column-projected sample)", "nl": "Enumeration Types are not usable in templates.\\nDescription\\n\\t \\n\\t\\t(last modified by Mariusz Felisiak)\\n\\t \\nThe new \u200benumeration types are great but can\'t be used in Django templates due to their being callable. For example this doesn\'t work:\\n{% if student.year_in_school == YearInSchool.FRESHMAN %}\\nThis is because YearInSchool, being a class, is callable, and Django Templates always call callables with no arguments. The call fails because the required value argument is missing.\\nThe easy solution would be to declare do_not_call_in_templates = True on the various Choices classes.\\n", "braincode": "TASK FixEnumTemplateUsage():\\n  SetAttribute[target=\\"Choices classes in django/db/models/enums.py\\", key=\\"do_not_call_in_templates\\", value=true]", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "The proposed source change can be represented as an ACTION with explicit target, attribute, and value. The basis cannot determine whether SetAttribute denotes a source edit, runtime reflection, or a declaration on every relevant class, so independently generated plans are likely to differ."}]\nReuse a change unchanged only if you can argue the specific issue raised against it doesn\'t apply. Revising an existing spec construct is `op: "revise"` with the same construct_name.\n\n\nSearcher\'s notes for this sprint:\n{\'search_mode\': \'folder\', \'python_notes\': "Python\'s primary structural contribution is its emphasis on \'concise, composable statements\', a \'small orthogonal core\', and the avoidance of \'deep nesting\'. For BrainCode, this implies favoring a flat, sequential, and highly readable syntax over deeply nested hierarchies (like complex JSON or LISP-like trees). This directly serves the **Determinism** KPI by limiting the structural variations possible for a single thought, and the **Interpretability** KPI by ensuring expressions can be easily read top-to-bottom by humans.", \'html_notes\': "HTML introduces the concept of \'separating content from context/attributes\'. The provided text highlights the example of \'email my mom vs. my professor\'—the core action (email) is the same, but the attributes (register, tone, recipient) differ. Borrowing this allows BrainCode to maintain a tiny, orthogonal set of core actions while using attributes to handle nuance. This maximizes **Expressivity** and **Coverage** without bloating the core vocabulary, while keeping the syntax highly **Interpretable**.", \'english_notes\': "English serves as the baseline for natural language expressivity, but structurally, BrainCode should borrow its concept of \'closed-class function words\' (e.g., conditionals, negation, connectives). By isolating logical routing into a strict, closed set of operators, BrainCode can achieve high **Expressivity** (handling complex logic) while preventing the grammar from expanding infinitely. This strict bounding is essential for **Determinism**.", \'formal_language_notes\': "The foundational takeaway from formal language theory is the necessity of a \'context-free-ish, parseable core over an ambiguous context-sensitive one.\' A context-free grammar guarantees that a statement means the same thing regardless of the surrounding text, eliminating the ambiguity inherent in natural language. This is the absolute bedrock for BrainCode\'s **Determinism** (ensuring independent users/LLMs converge on the same meaning) and **Interpretability** (allowing straightforward parsing without complex contextual inference).", \'relevant_precedents\': [\'**Parsel**: A language for composing hierarchical task decompositions for LLMs.\\n- **Expressivity**: Strong for multi-step dependency and causal chains, as it natively maps how sub-tasks feed into one another.\\n- **Coverage**: Broadly generalizes across LLM planning tasks.\\n- **Determinism**: High structural determinism (forces a specific dependency graph), though the leaf nodes rely on natural language.\\n- **Interpretability**: High; the dependency graph is easily readable.\\n- **Illustrative Request**: \\\'Fetch the user data and then format it as a table.\\\'\\n- **Comparable Expression (Parsel-style)**:\\n  `process_data: fetch -> format`\\n  `fetch: "Retrieve user data from API"`\\n  `format: "Format the retrieved data as a table"`\', \'**ReAct**: A framework that interleaves reasoning steps and actions.\\n- **Expressivity**: Excellent for temporal ordering, causal chains, and conditional reactions based on environment feedback.\\n- **Coverage**: Highly generalizable to any interactive agentic domain.\\n- **Determinism**: Moderate. The rigid `Thought/Action/Observation` structure is deterministic, but the free-text nature of the `Thought` step tolerates high stylistic variation (which BrainCode aims to fix).\\n- **Interpretability**: Extremely high for humans, reading like a clear execution log.\\n- **Illustrative Request**: \\\'Check the weather in Tokyo and email it to Bob.\\\'\\n- **Comparable Expression (ReAct-style)**:\\n  `Thought: I need to get Tokyo\\\'s weather first.`\\n  `Action: GetWeather[Tokyo]`\\n  `Observation: 75F and sunny.`\\n  `Thought: Now I need to email this observation to Bob.`\\n  `Action: SendEmail[Bob, "Tokyo Weather: 75F and sunny"]`\', "**Automatic Textbook Formalization**: A system for converting informal textbook content into structured, verifiable representations.\\n- **Expressivity**: Must be exceptionally high to capture complex semantic/mathematical meaning without losing nuance.\\n- **Coverage**: While domain-specific to textbooks, its methodology for handling \'content that resists formalization\' is highly relevant to BrainCode\'s edge cases.\\n- **Determinism**: High; the explicit goal is to create checkable, verifiable syntax from ambiguous prose.\\n- **Interpretability**: High for machines (verifiable), though potentially dense for humans depending on the underlying formal representation used."], \'new_source_suggestion\': None, \'_search_mode\': \'folder\'}\n\nReturn JSON: {"foundations_overview": "2-4 sentences for the spec\'s Foundations section: the grammar philosophy and how it draws on the inspiration languages", "inspiration_summary": "1-2 sentences citing which language inspired which choice", "summary": "1 sentence", "changes": [{"op": "add" | "revise", "construct_name": "...", "grammar": "...", "semantics": "...", "worked_example": {"nl": "...", "braincode": "..."}, "glossary_gloss": "...", "change_type": "MAJOR" | "MINOR" | "PATCH", "rationale": "...", "cites_source": "..." | null}, ...]}'}], 'model': 'claude-sonnet-5', 'system': 'You are the **Shaper** in the BrainCode syntax-creation loop. This prompt is used **only for\nSprint 0** (bootstrap) — the one-time setup sprint that establishes BrainCode\'s entire initial\nsyntax basis before regular sprints run. Regular sprints use a separate prompt,\n`shaper_system.md`, to propose a set of changes against an existing spec. Your provider rotates\neach sprint across Anthropic/OpenAI/Gemini (see config.yaml) — the loop deliberately wants a\ndiverse group composing the language; whichever provider the rotation assigns to sprint 0\ncomposes this initial basis.\n\nBrainCode is a formal syntax for human agent-requests and agentic reasoning (task decomposition):\nexpressive, broad-coverage, deterministic, interpretable, and independent of any one model\'s\nlinguistic style, judged against five pre-KPIs (Coverage, Expressivity, Determinism, Improvement,\nInterpretability). The Critic will simulate your basis on randomly sampled real tasks (web-agent\ntrajectories, household-robot instructions, chat requests) and judge each pre-KPI — design for\nthose, not for a single toy example.\n\nPropose an entire initial syntax **basis** — several foundational, composable constructs at once\n(aim for 3-6), each as a change with `op: "add"` and `change_type: "MAJOR"`, grounded in\nstructural inspiration from the Searcher\'s notes on a handful of reference languages (default:\nPython, HTML, English) plus formal-language-theory foundations. Every later sprint composes from\nwhat you establish here, so favor a small, orthogonal core over broad coverage — redundancy at\nthis stage compounds across every future sprint. Where the Searcher\'s notes include a comparable\nrendering from a precedent language, use it as a reference point for how compact and unambiguous\nyour own constructs should be.\n\nBefore finalizing the basis, verify it resolves every one of these — they\'re the standard places\na first-draft minimal grammar leaves gaps, and the Critic will check for exactly these, so leaving\none open just costs a wasted rework cycle:\n\n- **Evaluation order**: if one construct can nest inside another (e.g. an action as another\n  action\'s argument), state whether/when the inner one evaluates — don\'t leave it implicit.\n- **Operator precedence, associativity, and short-circuiting**: any construct with more than one\n  logical/boolean connective (and/or/not, etc.) needs an explicit precedence and evaluation-order\n  rule, not just a grammar listing the operators.\n- **Pure vs. effectful**: if anything usable as a condition/check can also be a side-effecting\n  action, say so explicitly and define what "true" means for it.\n- **Identifier scoping and reference resolution**: if names can refer to more than one kind of\n  thing (a task, a variable, a literal), state how a reader/parser disambiguates, and whether\n  self-reference/recursion/cycles between named units are allowed and what they mean.\n- **Data flow / binding**: if one step\'s output might need to feed a later step\'s input, either\n  provide a construct for that or explicitly scope it as future work.\n- **Iteration / collections**: real tasks range over lists of stops, files, search results. If the\n  basis has no way to express "for each" or "at least one of", say so explicitly in the\n  foundations overview as a known gap rather than letting the Critic discover it.\n- **Duplicate/conflicting modifiers**: if a construct accepts a set of key=value-style modifiers,\n  state what happens on a duplicate key.\n- **Lexical primitives**: identifiers, string/number literals, whitespace/comment handling, and\n  reserved words don\'t need a full grammar, but should be named so "strictly parseable" is a\n  claim the grammar actually earns.\n\nIf this is a rework (the user message says a previous attempt was sent back), the Critic\'s\n`required_changes` are your checklist — address every one explicitly.\n\nRespond in the JSON schema given inline in the user message for this request: an object with\n`foundations_overview` (2-4 sentences for the spec\'s Foundations section), `inspiration_summary`\n(1-2 sentences citing which language inspired which choice), `summary`, and a `changes` array —\neach change using the same shape as the regular per-sprint schema (`op`, `construct_name`,\n`grammar`, `semantics`, `worked_example`, `glossary_gloss`, `change_type`, `rationale`,\n`cites_source`). Every change must carry a complete `glossary_gloss` and `worked_example`.\n'}}
